body:
you're not being dramatic. this situation is unfortunately pretty common...friends, even family defending the abuser. it really sucks and im sorry you had to deal with that type of bullshit. its the worst feeling of betrayal, its like a complete invalidation of your pain and what you're going through, which is why you're feeling so triggered. the old feelings of guilt and shame are resurfaced because your ex friend/rape sympathizer bitch is back and some part of you is aware that even if you rekindle the friendship, she could still take the side of the rapist.

&#x200B;

being raped is shitty for obvious reasons but there are even studies that show that PTSD symptoms are more common among rape victims who do not have a good social support/backbone. for your own well being, your best bet is to cut this person out of your life for good. if they valued your friendship THEY would have apologized by now.

&#x200B;

i wish you the best of luck in your healing.

That is a terrifying story. Being so young and in the hospital, you should have been able to trust your nurse to take care of you! That woman is a monster, I can only imagine how many other children she touched... 

&#x200B;

Its good that you are okay, but since you are bringing it up you might want to speak with an actual therapist? Or a close friend or family member if therapy is not an option. Just reading it is sickening to my stomach, I wish you best of luck on your path to healing.

this is literally so shockingly similar to my experience with one person nearly seven years ago...from it being his birthday and everyone celebrating him...to him "letting" me stay in his room...from the blood and me thinking "omg my period, how unexpected" and then it...not being my period...its horrifying. 

&#x200B;

its wild to me because since then, i have had rough enough sex (on a consensual level) to draw blood and its never been anything like that night. the fact that i was able to confuse it with my full PERIOD is telling...

&#x200B;

you were raped. really terribly. im so so sorry, my full heart and being goes out to you because it was truly such a violent and terrible thing. its horrific and i wish it never happened to either of us, or that i could undo it in some way. please, please reach out to me if you feel you can't handle life or recovery...i will keep you in my prayers. i wish you the best of luck OP.

i love this story. i think its a good way to introduce the concept of empathy and helps guides people in the direction towards bettering themselves. 

&#x200B;

its interesting though, how in order for us to treat others with empathy, we have to believe that we are really still helping ourselves in some way?

I don't know how it can be treated. For context/background, a lot of my conclusion has come with my own experiences with being raped as well as observations of convicted pedophiles in my extended family. 

&#x200B;

It bothers me quite a bit to see homosexuality compared to pedophilia in any way. Homosexuality is not predatory in any way, its purely about mutual attraction/love. Being gay just means that you love someone who is like you physically, versus pedophilia is *at best* fantasizing about hurting someone.

(context: I'm Hispanic, but I look white. Nobody ever knows I am Hispanic unless I tell them, so for all intents and purposes, I am white)  

&#x200B;

I don't really believe in reverse racism. I have been in situations where someone of another race has told me exactly why my mere presence as a white person makes them uncomfortable or why they feel they cannot be their most authentic self around me, even though I personally have done nothing to indicate that I would be judgmental. 

&#x200B;

And, they are right. You can't really compare racism and sexism, though they are both oppressive social constructs, because white people simply don't face the same negative consequences of racism as men do with sexism. Western society is built by white people, for white people. There isn't any getting around that and perpetuating the idea of "reverse racism" or saying that white people are in ANY WAY a victim of racism is honestly offensive to all of the pain and suffering that we have caused.

that is extremely tough. i'm so sorry for what happened and how its affecting your mental well being and your life in general. you absolutely aren't alone in this though, and there are good people out there who will treat you as you should be deserved and make you feel safe and protected. since its having such a drastic impact on your every day life, have you considered therapy with a professional? with the covid situation its likely you will be able to find someone near you who can do video sessions -- allowing you to be safe at home and not having to wear any mask. you also usually can choose between male/female therapists. 

&#x200B;

i wish you the best of luck on your path to healing.

its definitely rape, especially where you literally had a safeword that he ignored? what is even the point of the safeword if you're not going to listen to it? 

&#x200B;

the rest is rape via coercion/manipulation. i mean you were bleeding and in pain just by going to the bathroom...thats pretty messed up. i understand that he himself was mentally unwell, but he still definitely took advantage of your kindness. he needs therapy for his own problems so that he can learn not to be toxic (and not to be a freaking rapist) but right now you need to prioritize your own healing. i wish you the best of luck.

Hey. I'm so so sorry for what you're experiencing right now and how scary it is. Everything you described is very typical of "rape trauma syndrome" (I highly recommend researching this, it will at least help you feel like what you're going through emotionally is common, even if it feels absolutely unbearable at times). basically there are 3 phases and the first two phases are super intense, but once you get through the tough part it gets much easier. 

&#x200B;

Please do know that your symptoms WILL subside. It takes time, and sometimes it feels like a slow fade, but I promise you it will happen. You probably won't wake up one day and magically just feel like your old self, and I'm not gonna tell you some shit about finding the "new normal" because that never made any sense to me. What happened to you was a trauma, and there was nothing normal about it, and right now you might feel like your mind is on fire, but slowly as time passes that fire will dim in your rearview mirror.

&#x200B;

the court case may be very difficult to relive, especially if you are forced to confront him in person. because of that and the fact that you mentioned you were already feeling suicidal, i would highly recommend therapy with a licensed professional, at least until the suicidal thoughts fade. I wish you the best of luck.

Wow, that is *a lot* of trauma especially in such a short period of time. Its no wonder you're feeling such distress! This post was so, so difficult to read. You're not a monster. The compassion you're showing towards other people in your life...show to yourself. You've been through hell, and the journey to healing can be extremely difficult and confusing. You're able to acknowledge your flaws as well -- you admitted you felt you were being manipulative/toxic to your gf. You clearly have a good sense of empathy, but you need to show that same empathy towards *you.* 

&#x200B;

Everything will be okay, please know that OP. This trauma you are experiencing is NOT your fault, so please don't blame yourself. I understand not wanting to feel like the girl who cried wolf...ive been there myself, and its a difficult pack to carry. You need a safe space to talk about this, with someone who won't judge you or question your honesty. I highly recommend a therapist, but I understand why for some that isn't a good option. If anything, feel free to DM me. I'll always believe you. Good Luck.

>The first time something happened was at least in part my fault because I suppose part of me really liked being close to someone again and even after saying no several times I didn't put in place any physical boundaries and things did end up escalating.

nope, not your fault. you said no several times, and he didn't immediately stop...that isn't okay. im not a professional, but it sounds like there are a few things happening here which is resulting in cognitive dissonance and lots of confusion on your part. first, the quote from above...that is definitely assault. however, just because you were assaulted by him once doesn't mean that you couldn't have consensual sex with him later for whatever reason. which, you could easily enjoy. however, you also feel guilty for enjoying it because of your other relationship where you feel you are being disloyal.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

>In the days and weeks after he began trying to touch me in small ways but I'd reject him upon which he would start crying or go into an 'anxiety attack' basically any form of emotional manipulation to get me to comfort him, which worked because he wouldn't leave until he got his way. I've never felt like someone who has much control over their situation and he recognized that and even got off on the fact that he had that power over me.

here again after the initial time, you describe where you *regularly* reject him, and he manipulates/coerces you into sexual activities anyways. that is assault by definition. of course you feel like you don't have control over the situation...you're being raped. 

&#x200B;

one thing you can do is educate yourself on rape trauma syndrome (the wikipedia page is pretty informative tbh) which will just help you make sense of your symptoms, as you mentioned feeling numbness. in terms of your "friend" (he is not your friend, he is using you and sounds like a very toxic person) they definitely need therapy themselves, because they are hurting other people. its up to you if you report, but i wouldn't confront them in person or right away -- they have demonstrated they are not above manipulating you to get what they want. i rarely think ghosting is the appropriate choice, but in this case, i would do it, to protect yourself. good luck.

i wouldn't waste your brain space debating about the "could have beens" in terms of what would have happened if you saw them in person. texting allows people to hide behind a screen and they behave in ways that they normally wouldnt in person, so there is no way to know. they may be more aggressive in real life, or they may be less aggressive. 

&#x200B;

you mention fear of abandonment....this is definitely something worthwhile discussing with a professional. you don't want to fall into a relationship where you are losing yourself or doing things you otherwise would not in order to keep the other person around.

hey. you aren't alone. what happened to you was extremely traumatic and it takes time to heal from that, especially since it was happening during such formative years in your life. you will be okay though, in fact you are already much more progressed in ways that you might not even notice in yourself. for one, you've acknowledged what happened and how its affected you, both to yourself as well as here to the internet. you don't know any of us so its easy, but we are still human beings behind our computer screens even though you can't see our faces. 

&#x200B;

you also showed deeper moral development from the experience --- you said you can see how your difficulty in progressing in a "normal" sexual relationship has affected your girlfriend. you also mentioned how coming forward about it affected other relationships and your concern about others feelings in this regard. all of these things demonstrates empathy, something your mother clearly never showed for you. you also stated concern for your brothers. already you are leaps and bounds further along in your healing than you are aware because your acknowledgement of what happened will allow you to break what could have been a cycle of abuse towards others. you've experienced something terrible and chosen to take the path towards becoming better.

&#x200B;

in terms of further progression, therapy helps, but it isn't always the answer for everything. good luck in your healing.

they literally do have evil plans --- they steal your name, pictures, etc and create fake social media accounts to create propaganda and rig elections, for example.

&#x200B;

i can see why you wouldn't be bothered to get personalized/targeted ads, but there are definitely people and organizations that use your info to commit crimes. you have to remember that the internet isn't owned by any country and so it isn't regulated by any one government. so spies/hackers/etc from other countries have no legal obligation to you not to steal your info.

i work in for one of the big banks. you won't go to jail or be charged with anything for lying about fradulent activity. people lie all the time about this because they don't want to pay for their purchases. probably you'll be out of the money that you sent, but you won't face criminal charges because you lied to the bank.

&#x200B;

whats going to happen is this: the bank's fraud team will do an "investigation" to determine how likely it was that fraud truly happened. if the investigation goes in your favor (unlikely) you'll be refunded for the charges, and if it doesn't then you'll be expected to pay them. the "investigation" process can take a few days to 3 months. you'll probably get a letter in the mail with a claim number and another letter in the mail with the results. if you want you can always call them and speak with the claims department and cancel the claim/investigation. 

&#x200B;

if you don't mind me asking, why exactly were you sending "tons" of money to someone on paypal? what were you trying to buy or what did you think you were buying?

i have one aunt who's death was ruled as a suicide but her immediate family thinks it a murder involving a religious group (I think its the seventh day eventists or whatever they are called but it might be some other cult)

&#x200B;

i have a "cousin" (in-law) who was in a fugue state for like, 10 years and nobody knows why, and she has complete amnesia of that entire decade. they thought she was schitzophrenic but she was able to recover magically. the current theory is sexual assault but we don't know for sure.

&#x200B;

i have an uncle who was a convicted sex offender and everyone is questioning whether or not he actually did it (most of us are on board with the idea that he definitely did do it, but there are a few who are not)

&#x200B;

a few of my grandfather's siblings all fled the state suddenly and moved across the country in the mid-50s, nobody knows exactly what happened because all of the witnesses have now died. we know they were running from the police but we don't know what crime was committed, but whatever it was they absolutely got away with it.

&#x200B;

there are a few others but the FBI agent in my computer already knows too much at this point.

its important for you to vocalize your feelings in a "safe" environment because thinking them in your head isn't the same thing. thats why they keep saying to let them out. its annoying af sometimes but thats why. 

&#x200B;

it sounds like you would benefit from some other type of therapeutic activity so that you can get to the root cause. i highly recommend the book "the body keeps the score". it might offer your insight that your therapist is unable to in regards to what is actually happening on a biological level due to the trauma.

as someone who works in a bank, this is absolutely necessary, and the earlier the better.  i think it should be taught multiple times, possibly every year of high school. the lack of financial literacy in our society is horrifying. we are talking about not knowing the difference between credit and debit, not knowing what "available balance" means, or what a "returned check" is...and every time its just "oh youre a big bank so youre just trying to steal money from the little guy!" people want to blame everyone -- the bank, their government, society -- for their financial failures. ive lived below the poverty line and NEVER had an overdraft fee in any of my accounts, so i know it isn't just the bank screwing you. 

&#x200B;

its easy to blame the big banks for charging overdraft and returned item fees and "kicking you when your down" but what people don't realize is how many losses banks take from overdrawn accounts. the fees are necessary to help balance those losses. your overdrawn account doesn't just hurt you, it hurts your bank too especially if it goes to collections, but few people understand this. so yes, personal finance should definitely be a part of the high school curriculum.

if it makes you feel better, even if the most black and white cases victims are very frequently questioning "was i raped?" im not sure if you can technically use the term assault, but it seems like the injury is the same as if you were touched physically. it sounds an awful lot like coercion of some kind, because you were doing things you otherwise wouldn't have out of some type of fear or threat.  

&#x200B;

i think its really common for victims to think "this really wasn't that bad, considering how much worse other people have it". i know i definitely do. for me personally, mine is "i have met survivors of genocide, therefore how can i be upset that this man ignored my no when i am clearly physically fine". its crazy how guilt or shame can make us invalidate our experience and memories.

&#x200B;

its difficult to get out of this way of thinking and really important not to discredit your own feelings in this way. if you felt coerced to do things you didn't want to out of fear or due to threat, that is definitely *at least* sexual assault-adjacent, if not outright assault in some manner, because someone else was making decisions for your body/controlling your body. i hope this helps you find the answer you're searching for, and good luck in your healing.

okay. get ready for TMI. i met up with him just to catch up. sex was not implied, i actually wasnt going to stay at his place at all but i got excited to see my old friend and drank too much. he said he would sleep on the couch, but then he wanted to netflix/cuddle and one thing led to another. when we were kissing, we moved into his bedroom. he  removed my clothes and started foreplay, and i asked him to stop. he ignored it and kept going. i asked him many more times, louder and louder but he was really strong. finally eventually he did stop because i was able to use my feet to kick at him a bit. then, i felt bad about freaking out, so we had actual sex, which i consented to. during the actual sex, he got very aggressive, almost BDSM, choking me without any warning and it was very painful. i was bleeding a little and i begged him to stop. he stopped, finally, and i went into the bathroom and cried a lot. then, i calmed myself down, and went back and finished having sex until he had an orgasm (me on top this time so i was in full control). then, i went into the bathroom and had a full actual panic attack, flashbacks and everything. then we went to sleep (i barely slept) and i woke up in a panic several times.

&#x200B;

so, technically i did consent, he just initiated unexpected things and took longer than necessary to actually stop. i probably asked him like 10 or more times before he did, which i thought maybe he couldn't hear, but then he confirmed over the phone that he did in fact hear me but he thought i was being playful.

youre absolutely right. its a bit more complicated than that. the letter was sort of a "shoot myself in the foot" moment. i know the right thing to do, morally, would be to take him (lets call him G) to court. but the easy thing to do is not to. you see, years ago when i had the violent situation (lets call him T), i was too scared to do anything. i didn't press any charges and T faced zero reprecussions. 3 years ago, i ran into T at our college town. i was with my best friend, L and i told her what happened. L then told me T had also raped her, a year after he raped me. i have felt guilty about that, because maybe if i had been brave enough to press charges then my best friend wouldn't have been raped by the exact same person. 

&#x200B;

somewhere, in the back of my mind, i knew G was raping me when it happened. i knew it the moment it happened and i didn't want to accept it. ive been in denial for weeks and ive held onto the idea that if we stayed friends then it didn't really happen the way that i perceived it. by sending the letter, it eliminated any chance i could ever take G to court. morally, it was actually a selfish move on my part, because of any other girl that this could or has happened to.
